      "content": "Let's get down to business, shall we? The math behind it is quite straightforward. The interest is calculated using the formula: Interest = Principal x Rate x Time. In this case, Time is the number of months.\n\nFor example, if you have a $5,000 balance and an interest rate of 6%, the interest for the first month would be:\n\nInterest = $5,000 x 0.06 x 1 = $300\n\nSo, in the first month, $300 of your payment would go towards interest, and $4,700 would go towards the principal.\n\nNow, let's say you make a payment of $5,000 (the full amount). The interest for the next month would be calculated on the new balance of $4,700, so:\n\nInterest = $4,700 x 0.06 x 1 = $283.20\n\nIn this case, $283.20 of your payment would go towards interest, and $4,716.80 would go towards the principal.\n\nAs you can see, the interest is calculated on the outstanding balance, so it's not always a straightforward 50/50 split. But don't worry, the math is on your side - with consistent",
